L49: share and change all versions of a program--to make sure it remains free
L50: software for all its users.  We, the Free Software Foundation, use the
L51: GNU General Public License for most of our software; it applies also to
L52: any other work released this way by its authors.  You can apply it to
L53: your programs, too.
L54:   When we speak of free software, we are referring to freedom, not
L55: price.  Our General Public Licenses are designed to make sure that you
L56: have the freedom to distribute copies of free software (and charge for
L57: them if you wish), that you receive source code or can get it if you
L58: want it, that you can change the software or use pieces of it in new
L59: free programs, and that you know you can do these things.
L60:   To protect your rights, we need to prevent others from denying you
L61: these rights or asking you to surrender the rights.  Therefore, you have
L62: certain responsibilities if you distribute copies of the software, or if
L63: you modify it: responsibilities to respect the freedom of others.
L64:   For example, if you distribute copies of such a program, whether
L65: gratis or for a fee, you must pass on to the recipients the same
L66: freedoms that you received.  You must make sure that they, too, receive
L67: or can get the source code.  And you must show them these terms so they
L68: know their rights.
L69: 
L70:   Developers that use the GNU GPL protect your rights with two steps:
L71: (1) assert copyright on the software, and (2) offer you this License
L72: giving you legal permission to copy, distribute and/or modify it.
L73:   For the developers' and authors' protection, the GPL clearly explains
L74: that there is no warranty for this free software.  For both users' and
L75: authors' sake, the GPL requires that modified versions be marked as
L76: changed, so that their problems will not be attributed erroneously to
L77: authors of previous versions.
L78:   Some devices are designed to deny users access to install or run
L79: modified versions of the software inside them, although the manufacturer
L80: can do so.  This is fundamentally incompatible with the aim of
L81: protecting users' freedom to change the software.  The systematic
L82: pattern of such abuse occurs in the area of products for individuals to
L83: use, which is precisely where it is most unacceptable.  Therefore, we
L84: have designed this version of the GPL to prohibit the practice for those
L85: products.  If such problems arise substantially in other domains, we
L86: stand ready to extend this provision to those domains in future versions
L87: of the GPL, as needed to protect the freedom of users.
L88:   Finally, every program is threatened constantly by software patents.
L89: States should not allow patents to restrict development and use of
L90: software on general-purpose computers, but in those that do, we wish to
L91: avoid the special danger that patents applied to a free program could
L92: make it effectively proprietary.  To prevent this, the GPL assures that
L93: patents cannot be used to render the program non-free.
